==English==

===Etymology===
From late 17th century {{bor|en|fr|effronterie|}}, from {{m|fr|effronté||shameless, insolent}}, from {{der|en|fro|esfronté|}}, from {{der|en|VL.|*exfrontātus}}. Compare {{cog|la|effrōns||barefaced}}, from the prefix {{m|la|ex-||from}} + {{m|la|frōns||forehead}} (English: {{l|en|front}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪˈfɹʌntəɹi/|/ɛˈfɹʌntəɹi/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-effrontery.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[insolent|Insolent]] and [[shameless]] [[audacity]].
#: {{ux|en|We even had the '''effrontery''' to suggest that he should leave the country.}}
#* '''1859''', ''The Christian Remembrancer'' (volumes 37-38, page 250)
#*: Let not the Englishman in Scotland believe that the undoffed hat, the curt reply, the apparent assumption of equality, all spring from deliberate '''effrontery''', and are wholly beyond the reach of southern influence.
# {{lb|en|countable}} An [[act]] of insolent and shameless audacity.
#: {{ux|en|Any refusal to salute the president shall be counted as an '''effrontery'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=Justin O. Schmidt|title=The Sting of the Wild|publisher=Johns Hopkins University Press,|isbn=978-1-4214-1928-2|page=92
|passage=All was going as planned until the bag hit a snag and failed to surround the entire nest. This '''effrontery''' was too much. The wasps exploded off the nest at me.}}

